    "durationMs": 172961,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified. Here's what I built:\n\n**Stack**: Spring Boot 3.4.5 / Java 21, Spring Data JPA, Flyway, PostgreSQL 16 — all wired to `DATABASE_URL` (converted from PostgreSQL URL to JDBC by an `EnvironmentPostProcessor`, no hard-coded credentials).\n\n**Endpoints** (all verified against the live server):\n- `POST /links` → 201 with `{\"code\",\"url\",\"clicks\":0}`, URL returned unchanged; generates 8-char codes from the allowed alphabet; duplicate custom code → 409, invalid/missing url, invalid code, or malformed JSON → 422 with an `error` field\n- `GET /links/{code}` → 200, unknown → 404 with `error`\n- `GET /r/{code}` → 302 with `Location` exactly equal to the stored URL; clicks incremented via an atomic SQL `UPDATE` (exact under concurrency)\n- `GET /links` → 200 `{\"items\":[...],\"total\"}` in ascending creation order\n\n**Persistence**: Flyway migration `V1__init.sql` creates the `links` table with a unique index on `code`; `ddl-auto=none`. I verified a kill/restart cycle preserved data and click counts.\n\n**Operations**: `sh start.sh` runs the prebuilt `target/app.jar` in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000` (confirmed reachable on the non-loopback interface), with Flyway applying pending migrations at startup before serving. The README explains startup. Test servers were stopped and the database left clean (empty `links`, migrations applied)."
